> **Summary:** Framework for determining when multiple contact reasons from the same customer interaction must be logged as separate conversations versus treated as a single interaction. Use this when a ticket contains more than one customer issue or question.

## Overview

This framework determines when multiple [[Contact Reasons]] from the same interaction must be categorized separately, and when a contact reason does not need its own individual conversation logged.

**Used in:** Kustomer
**Applies to:** CH agents handling tickets where multiple topics arise

---

## Core Rule: Separate vs. Single Interaction

| Scenario | Treatment |
|---|---|
| Multiple issues arrived **together in the same message** | **Single interaction** — one conversation, primary contact reason logged |
| Multiple issues arrived **sequentially** (one after another, in separate exchanges) | **Separate interactions** — split into individual conversations, each categorized independently |

---

## Examples

### Two Separate Interactions (split required)

A customer asks a WISMO question → agent shares tracking info → customer then asks a product question.

Even if both exchanges happened within a short time window, these are **two separate interactions**:
1. Log and categorize the WISMO question first
2. Split into a new conversation for the product question

### Single Interaction (no split needed)

A customer sends one email asking about separated shipment tracking *and* asking about the weight limit of her stools.

Both requests came in together in the same message → this is a **single interaction**. Log one conversation with the primary contact reason.

---

## Why This Matters

- Reduces unnecessary categorization work when customers bundle multiple questions around a primary issue
- Ensures important concerns and resolutions are still tracked appropriately
- Logging an additional conversation to capture important data or ongoing concerns is still permitted and encouraged when the situation warrants it

